fact WorkItem(id: "WI-715", description: some(value: "anthill-todo test `claim_completes_when_description_has_unbalanced_paren` (cmd_claim_unbalanced_paren_test.rs) is LOAD-SENSITIVE and fails spuriously under parallel test execution: it spawns the CLI and panics if it has not exited within a hard 10-SECOND wall-clock budget, but the operation takes ~2.7s on an idle machine -- barely 3.7x headroom. Under `scripts/test.sh` (many test binaries in parallel) the box reaches load average 13-33 and the same run takes 10-20s, so the test fails. Observed while delivering WI-710: it failed in the full suite, passed 3/3 standalone on a quiet machine (2.67/2.73/2.78s), and an interleaved A/B on an idle box showed NO load-time regression from WI-710 (medians 2.61/2.74s vs baseline 2.70/2.77s) -- i.e. the failure is purely scheduling noise, and the wall-clock budget is the defect. The test exists to guard a real past bug (a `find_fact_block` infinite loop on an unbalanced `(` in a description), so the GUARD must stay -- but a wall-clock deadline is the wrong instrument for 'did not hang': it conflates a hang with a busy CI machine, and it will keep producing false red. Fix directions: (a) raise the budget substantially (60s+) so it still catches an infinite loop but not a loaded box; (b) better, assert on COMPLETION rather than elapsed time -- the regression was a non-terminating loop, so any finite exit passes, and a generous timeout is only a backstop against hanging the suite forever. Acceptance: the test cannot fail on a machine that is merely busy; it still fails if find_fact_block regresses to a non-terminating loop; cargo test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T22:46:23Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-715", author: "user", content: "Fixed per direction (b): pass/fail decided by process COMPLETION + exit status; wall-clock is only a finite backstop against a non-terminating regression, no longer a performance deadline. Backstop set to 20s (user chose 20s; 120s was flagged as too big). Verified green: full workspace suite (74/74 binaries) passed twice; the test itself completed in ~1.5s standalone and 1.56s under full-suite PARALLEL load on this box (~13x headroom under 20s). TRADEOFF ON RECORD: WI's worst-observed loaded time was 10-20s, so 20s has a thinner margin than 120s -- if it flakes again under heavier load, bump the backstop (the comment + panic message tell a maintainer to do exactly that). The finite backstop preserves the original infinite-loop guard: an unbalanced-paren find_fact_block regression never terminates and still trips it.", at: "2026-07-13T22:46:17Z")
